1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,000
This board is an AI accelerator
I built solo.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
It just booted itself
from its own flash.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
It's called SplitSys.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,500
It's running a real public language model,
stories260K.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,500
Every linear layer runs on this chip;
attention and softmax stay on the laptop.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,000
About two seconds per token,
and almost all of that is the serial link.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
On-chip, a token takes
295 microseconds.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,500
When a model writes one token at a time,
each matrix multiply is a single row tall.

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,000
In a square 8×8 array,
seven of eight rows sit idle.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Here's FULL mode at decode size:
only the top row lights up.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,500
6.95% utilization,
from the hardware counters.

12
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,000
Now the same 64 multipliers rewire
at runtime into 4×16.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The bottom half computes more output columns:
14.40%.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Across all four transformer matmuls,
that's 2.02 to 2.07×, measured on the board.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,500
Before the board session,
I wrote a cycle model of the chip.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:09,000
On 773 hardware runs,
it predicted all twelve counters exactly,

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
and every output matched
my golden model bit for bit.

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The split isn't always better.

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,500
At M = 64, STRIP is only slightly ahead
on these benchmarks,

20
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:27,000
and on 31 of 97 random shapes FULL was faster,
because 4×16 tiles pad worse.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
So the model picks the mode
for every job.

22
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,500
It costs 25% more operand bandwidth
and 2,243 more lookup tables,

23
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:42,000
mostly a wider requantizer.
I measured that too.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
A button press runs the on-board self-test:
one FULL and one STRIP job.

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
This isn't a speed claim against a CPU or GPU.
It's about making every multiplier count.

26
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
SplitSys. Built solo
at Cal Hacks 13.0.
